  "id": "Compound::DB00443 - Disease::DOID:4481",
  "verbalization": "Betamethasone, through its pharmacological relationship with other glucocorticoids, is implicated in the treatment of allergic rhinitis. The evidence suggests that Betamethasone, as a therapeutic glucocorticoid, is linked to Methylprednisolone, which in turn treats allergic rhinitis. This pathway is supported by the shared ancestry of Betamethasone and Methylprednisolone with 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroids and Therapeutic Glucocorticoids, indicating a common biochemical class. The indirect relationship between Betamethasone and allergic rhinitis is further reinforced by its role in treating asthma, which is then treated by Methylprednisolone, creating a cascading effect through the glucocorticoid class. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Betamethasone to be repurposed for allergic rhinitis due to its shared mechanisms with other glucocorticoids, which are known to modulate immune responses and reduce inflammation.",
